message.content:
I don’t have direct access to the VirusTotal API from here, so I can’t fetch the data for you.  
However, here’s how you could do it yourself using the VirusTotal Public API (or the Enterprise API if you have one):

```bash
# Example using curl (replace YOUR_API_KEY with your actual key)
curl -X GET "https://www.virustotal.com/api/v3/domains/example.org/relationships/subdomains" \
     -H "x-apikey: YOUR_API_KEY" \
     -G --data-urlencode "limit=10" \
     --data-urlencode "continuation_cursor=abc"
```

The response will be a JSON object that looks roughly like this:

```json
{
  "data": [
    {
      "id": "subdomain1.example.org",
      "type": "domain",
      "attributes": {
        "last_analysis_stats": { ... },
        "last_analysis_date": 1700000000,
        ...
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "subdomain2.example.org",
      "type": "domain",
      "attributes": { ... }
    }
    // up to 10 items
  ],
  "links": { ... },
  "meta": {
    "continuation_cursor": "next_cursor_value"
  }
}
```

If you only want the IDs and any context attributes (e.g., `last_analysis_stats`, `last_analysis_date`), you can parse the JSON and extract those fields. In Python, for example:

```python
import requests

url = "https://www.virustotal.com/api/v3/domains/example.org/relationships/subdomains"
headers = {"x-apikey": "my_api"}
params = {"limit": 10, "continuation_cursor": "abc"}

resp = requests.get(url, headers=headers, params=params)
data = resp.json()

for item in data.get("data", []):
    print("ID:", item["id"])
    # Print any context attributes you care about
    attrs = item.get("attributes", {})
    print("  last_analysis_stats:", attrs.get("last_analysis_stats"))
    print("  last_analysis_date:", attrs.get("last_analysis_date"))
```

That will give you just the IDs and the selected context attributes for each related subdomain. If you need further help parsing or filtering the response, let me know!
